  "findings": [
    "Both display variants consistently show sourceRGB/A(no contact)/B(manual-phase contact), using shared source cameras/object geometry and the same display ROI for both methods. Closeup makes finger differences more visible while context retains the whole scene.",
    "The sampled carrying frames show small A/B changes, mainly in the supported right hand; no clear grasp-fidelity superiority of B is established from these source comparisons.",
    "The far/left hand is frequently hidden by the estimated orange object. Its129 usable-carry finger states are frozen in both variants; static appearance cannot demonstrate recovered hidden articulation. A depth-tested overlay can conceal an estimated hand inside the estimated object, so the independent skin-depth conflict must remain in the conclusion.",
    "At110 and210 sourceRGB still shows carrying while the caption says attractionOFF. This is object-pose uncertainty abstention, not a physical release label or a missing prediction.",
    "At250/251 the admission switch is visible without an obvious large static pose discontinuity in the adjacent rendered pair; this one pair does not override the full-clip motion-guard failures.",
    "The sampled285/287/290 sequence shows hands opening/withdrawing in both methods, without an obvious new B-only lock at this display resolution. Exact release timing and hidden finger positions remain unverified.",
    "Neither lower anchor residuals nor visual mesh overlap confirms correct contact, penetration truth, calibrated scale or competition scores.",
    "The independent numerical evaluation still fails11 supported finger-motion edges,14 supported wrist-motion edges and the fixed usable-carry right worst-skin nonregression guard(+2.246mm vs+2mm limit)."
  ],
